# Required for the prod release. Generate with: mix phx.gen.secret
SECRET_KEY_BASE=

# Public hostname (URL generation / WebSocket origin check).
# Local verification: localhost. Deployment: the vhost name.
PHX_HOST=localhost

# HTTP listen port (default 4000; keep in sync with the compose ports
# mapping).
# PORT=4000

# Plain-HTTP mode for VPN-only deployment without a TLS-terminating
# reverse proxy (config/prod.exs, 2026-07-26): "true" disables force_ssl
# and the Secure cookie flag at BUILD time and switches generated URLs /
# check_origin to http://PHX_HOST:PORT at runtime. docker compose wires
# this single value to both; a mismatch aborts boot. Tokens and cookies
# ride unencrypted HTTP — only use inside a trusted VPN, never with a
# public reverse proxy.
# KAOIRO_PLAIN_HTTP=true

# Host IP the compose port mapping publishes on (default 127.0.0.1 =
# loopback only, for a same-host reverse proxy). Direct VPN deployments
# set the host's VPN-side interface IP, e.g. 192.168.0.100.
# KAOIRO_PUBLISH_IP=127.0.0.1

# Bind IP override (issue #139). :prod (release) only — dev (mix
# phx.server) always binds loopback (127.0.0.1, config/dev.exs) and
# ignores this, since dev.exs also hardcodes a publicly-known
# secret_key_base that must never be reachable off loopback. Unset
# keeps prod's default (all interfaces). Accepts an IPv4/IPv6 literal.
# KAOIRO_BIND_IP=0.0.0.0

# Socket auth (ADR-0011 / issue #28 / issue #138). This file feeds the
# RELEASE image, which evaluates auth in :prod — there an unset list is
# FAIL-CLOSED on every socket. The "dev mode" relaxation applies only to
# `mix` runs in :dev/:test, never to what compose starts:
#   KAOIRO_CLIENT_TOKENS unset -> client connections are REJECTED in any
#     env (fail-closed): the dashboard needs a token even for local use,
#     so a misconfigured deploy is locked rather than wide-open as
#     operator.
#   KAOIRO_WRAPPER_TOKENS unset -> :prod accepts ONLY server-minted
#     signed tokens (the runner spawn path, ADR-0024); pair auth is off
#     and anything unsigned is rejected. A runner-only deployment can
#     leave this unset (2026-08-02 revision). Only :dev/:test disable
#     wrapper auth entirely (any wrapper connects).
#   KAOIRO_RUNNER_TOKENS unset -> :prod rejects every runner join with
#     `unauthorized` (per host_id, ADR-0023). scripts/dogfood.sh runs
#     the release image, so it mints an entry here when it finds none.
# Set CLIENT (or OAuth) + RUNNER at minimum — a release started without
# them accepts nobody.
# KAOIRO_WRAPPER_TOKENS=lab-pc-1.ao:wrapper-token,...
# KAOIRO_RUNNER_TOKENS=lab-pc-1:runner-token,...
# KAOIRO_CLIENT_TOKENS=admin-token:admin,client-token:operator,viewer-token:viewer
#
# Roles (ADR-0050 D2): admin > operator > viewer. DECLARE AT LEAST ONE
# admin — here or on an OAuth allow-list line. admin is grantable ONLY
# through config, never from inside the app, so config edits are also the
# only recovery route from a zero-admin deployment. The two routes differ:
# an allow-list line takes effect on the next connect/refresh (the file is
# re-read live, ADR-0042 / issue #170), while a change to this variable
# needs a restart. Existing operators are NOT auto-promoted (マスター決裁
# 2026-08-14). The server logs "no usable admin" at boot while none is
# usable — an admin line on a provider without credentials is configured
# but grants nothing, and still triggers the warning.
# An optional third field names the token's kaoiro user (issue #197
# マスター決裁 2026-08-09 #1) — the display_name a first login seeds:
#   KAOIRO_CLIENT_TOKENS=admin-token:admin:Owner,client-token:operator:Ops Bot

# Dashboard OAuth login (ADR-0042). Independent of KAOIRO_CLIENT_TOKENS:
# with tokens unset the dashboard offers OAuth only, with both set it
# offers both (GET /session/auth-methods drives that UI).
#
# A provider is offered only when its WHOLE set is present; a half-filled
# pair silently disables it and is warned about at boot. The redirect URI
# is derived from PHX_HOST/PORT as
# {scheme}://{host}[:{port}]/auth/{provider}/callback — register exactly
# that value at the provider.
#
# Google requires an https redirect URI for anything but localhost, so it
# CANNOT be used together with KAOIRO_PLAIN_HTTP=true. GitHub and
# Nextcloud accept http redirect URIs.
# KAOIRO_OAUTH_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
# KAOIRO_OAUTH_GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
# KAOIRO_OAUTH_GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=
# KAOIRO_OAUTH_GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=
# KAOIRO_OAUTH_NEXTCLOUD_CLIENT_ID=
# KAOIRO_OAUTH_NEXTCLOUD_CLIENT_SECRET=
# KAOIRO_OAUTH_NEXTCLOUD_BASE_URL=https://cloud.example.org

# Who the OAuth logins above may authenticate as. A text file, one entry
# per line, re-read on every connect/refresh (no restart needed to revoke):
#
#   provider:identifier[:role]
#
#   provider   google | github | nextcloud
#   identifier google: e-mail (case-insensitive), github: login,
#              nextcloud: user id (both compared verbatim)
#   role       viewer | operator | admin, optional, defaults to viewer.
#              An admin line only counts while its provider has complete
#              credentials — an admin on a provider you never configured
#              can log in nowhere.
#
# Blank lines and lines starting with `#` are ignored; there is no
# trailing-comment syntax. A malformed line is skipped with a warning.
# UNSET OR UNREADABLE REJECTS EVERY OAUTH LOGIN (fail-closed) — a
# configured provider without this file authenticates nobody. Mount it
# read-only into the container.
#
#   google:master@example.com:admin
#   google:ops@example.com:operator
#   github:ao
# KAOIRO_OAUTH_ALLOWLIST_PATH=/etc/kaoiro/oauth-allowlist.txt

# Whether agents may observe the server's registered users (ADR-0021 F6-8
# allows only `id` / `kind` / `display_name` / `role`) in their directory
# response. NOT a presence signal: the set is every user in the persisted
# ledger whose role still resolves, so it keeps listing people who logged
# out long ago, and says nothing about who is connected right now.
# Spelled out here because THE DEFAULT IS OPEN and appeared nowhere in
# config until issue #233: a deployment that never mentions this variable
# IS exposing users.
#
#   unset      -> open  (issue #197 制約節「原則見える」, as a CONFIG
#                        default rather than an implementation default)
#   true       -> open
#   false      -> closed
#   any other  -> closed (fail-closed on a typo / "0" / "no")
#
# Closed means agents receive an empty `users` list, indistinguishable
# from "no user is registered". Parser: `Users.expose_to_agents_default/1`.
# KAOIRO_EXPOSE_USERS_TO_AGENTS=true

# Sprite overlay dir inside the container (pair with the compose volume).
# The server only READS this dir now — pack extraction goes to
# KAOIRO_PERSONA_CACHE_DIR (below), so a `:ro` mount cold-starts fine
# (ADR-0046). A missing dir warns, serves an empty manifest, and disables
# the watcher; the server never creates it.
# KAOIRO_PERSONA_DIR=/personas

# Where persona packs are extracted (ADR-0046). Contents are derived from
# the zips, so losing them only costs one re-extraction — but the server
# must be able to create and write this root at boot or it FAILS FAST.
# Unset falls back to a tmp path namespaced by the ingest dir's hash,
# which a container recreation wipes. The bundled docker-compose.yaml
# already points it at the kaoiro-state volume, so a vanilla dogfood.sh
# run needs no override here. Give each server process its own root —
# sharing one across processes is not supported.
# KAOIRO_PERSONA_CACHE_DIR=/var/lib/kaoiro/persona-cache

# Common-footer directory (ADR-0045). The server reads `system-footer.md`
# and `user-footer.md` directly under it — those exact names only, no
# per-persona variants — and rebuilds the prompt footer when either
# changes, no restart needed.
#
#   system-footer.md  replaces the built-in default COMPLETELY. The
#                     shipped default is a real file you can read:
#                     server/priv/footers/system-footer.md (also inside
#                     the release image). Absent or empty keeps the
#                     built-in.
#   user-footer.md    appended after the system footer. Absent or empty
#                     adds nothing.
#
# UNSET DISABLES both (built-in footer only). Deliberately separate from
# KAOIRO_PERSONA_DIR: that root's default lives inside the repo, so
# operational footers put there would end up in git and in the docker
# build context — and pack distribution is a different concern from
# prompt policy anyway. Mount this one READ-ONLY — the server never writes
# here and never creates the directory, so it must exist before boot
# (enabling it later takes a restart). Keep the two files out of git:
# they are per-environment operational config, like this .env.
# KAOIRO_FOOTER_DIR=/etc/kaoiro/footers

# Restart-surviving state paths. The bundled docker-compose.yaml already
# sets these via `environment:` + a `kaoiro-state` named volume, so a
# vanilla dogfood.sh run needs no override here. Only set these when
# running outside compose (mix phx.server on the host, an alternative
# orchestrator) — point them at a directory the runtime user can write.
# Unset falls back to the container's /tmp, which is wiped on
# `docker compose down` and takes the offline-agent list with it.
# KAOIRO_SESSION_POINTERS_PATH=/var/lib/kaoiro/session_pointers.dets
# KAOIRO_AGENT_DIRECTORY_PATH=/var/lib/kaoiro/agent_directory.dets
# KAOIRO_PERMISSION_MODES_PATH=/var/lib/kaoiro/permission_modes.dets
# KAOIRO_CLEAR_WATERMARKS_PATH=/var/lib/kaoiro/clear_watermarks.dets
# KAOIRO_SESSION_STARTS_PATH=/var/lib/kaoiro/session_starts.dets
# KAOIRO_INGRESS_ORDER_PATH=/var/lib/kaoiro/ingress_order.dets
# KAOIRO_DELIVERY_STATES_PATH=/var/lib/kaoiro/delivery_states.dets
# KAOIRO_TOKEN_DENYLIST_PATH=/var/lib/kaoiro/token_denylist.dets
# KAOIRO_USERS_PATH=/var/lib/kaoiro/users.dets
